<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Code of Ur-Nammu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> is the oldest known surviving law code, issued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> by Ur-Nammu or his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a>. Written in Sumerian, it prescribed monetary fines for many offences. It is a key document of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>'s legal and administrative order.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu.jpg" alt="Code of Ur-Nammu" width="211" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Istanbul Archaeology Museums · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…First Dynasty, though left off the Sumerian King List, exercised considerable influence in the region in the time before the dynasty of Akkad, and the prologue of the <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b> boasts 'He slew Nam-ha-ni the ensi of Lagash', a measure of how much the city still counted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b>, some three centuries older than Hammurabi's, punished many offences with monetary fines rather than physical penalties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ur-Nammu, who ruled c. 2047–2030 BCE by one chronology and c. 2112–2094 BCE by another, built temples including the Ziggurat of Ur, improved agriculture by irrigation and issued the <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b>, which precedes the Code of Hammurabi by 300 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier law collections in Sumerian and Akkadian survive, among them the <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b> of Ur, the Code of Lipit-Ishtar of Isin and the Laws of Eshnunna, and like Hammurabi's they purport to have been written by rulers; statements of other rulers suggest the custom was widespread.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
